            <ArticleTitle>Granulomatous gastritis: a diagnostic dilemma?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e222-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Granulomatous inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract is an uncommon entity; an aetiopathogenic diagnosis can be reached only by combining the morphological examination with clinical and laboratory investigations. We report two cases of granulomatous gastritis: a 27-year-old woman presenting with weight loss and a 55-year-old woman presenting with epigastric pain and vomiting. Upper oesophagastroduodenoscopy in these cases showed antral hyperaemia and histopathology showed non-caseating gastric granulomatous inflammation. Both the cases were extensively worked-up for possible tuberculosis (TB) as the patients lived in an endemic area, before starting steroids for the possibility of Crohn's disease (CD) . The first patient improved but the second patient had a flare of underlying undiagnosed TB. Granulomatous gastritis present a diagnostic challenge for treating physicians because of similar clinical, laboratory and endoscopical features between CD and intestinal TB.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
